<MainLayout>

  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Research Foundation</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A program of many small investigations into movement, perception, and architecture.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The Premise</h2>
    <p>
      Human consciousness and movement rest on three-dimensional perception, articulated by
      two hemispheres in a relation of interactive reflexivity. If that is so, then a machine
      built on the same principle — two complementary processing modules coordinated by an
      executive module that perceives behavior and adjusts it — is worth testing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dual Brain Robotics is that test. The research program asks a narrow, answerable
      question: does a bilateral, field-centered architecture move differently from a
      traditional linear, point-to-point controller?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>R&D Program 1.0</h2>
    <p>
      The first program is a set of <strong>60 simulations</strong> comparing a traditional
      linear, point-to-point control robot with a DBRA robot. The two machines receive
      identical sensors, identical actuator limits, and identical compute; only the
      architecture differs.
    </p>
    <p>The 60 simulations are organized into six families:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Linear warehousing — routine picks, pallet returns, and sorting</li>
      <li>Static and dynamic obstacle fields — detours, crossing traffic, sudden hazards</li>
      <li>Terrain — stairs, gaps, puddles, and uneven ground</li>
      <li>Interception and moving targets — prediction under motion</li>
      <li>Uncertainty and competing goals — incomplete information</li>
      <li>Reflexive, self-generated scenarios — the robot's own account of its behavior</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The program has two levels. <strong>Level 1</strong> is the individual simulations.
      <strong>Level 2</strong> is the cumulative database they feed, built on one set of
      standards so that results can be compared and analyzed together.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>
      The program is deliberately built from many small investigations rather than one grand
      experiment. Each simulation is designed in advance, with its hypothesis declared before
      it runs, and each is reported whether or not the hypothesis holds.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The baseline is a competent, modern autonomous system — never a weakened version of one.</li>
      <li>Both machines share the same sensors, limits, and tools; only the architecture differs.</li>
      <li>All tools are free and open, and every run records its seed and code version.</li>
      <li>Some scenarios are designed so that the DBRA robot is expected to lose. Those cases are reported as plainly as the others.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What Each Simulation Produces</h2>
    <p>Every simulation delivers five things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The run itself, with its seed and code version recorded</li>
      <li>Data and charts</li>
      <li>A record in the cumulative database</li>
      <li>A one-page report</li>
      <li>A one-minute video overview</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Taken together, the intention is a preponderance of evidence — many small results
      accumulating into a case for continued research, rather than a single dramatic claim.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      The program began in November 2026. The design phase is underway, and the first
      simulations are being run as their designs are approved. Results will be presented here
      as they become available.
    </p>
    <p>Detailed simulation presentations are in preparation.</p>
  </section>

